Ignore a resent copy of a packet that was already polled.

IHS_SessionPacketsWindowAdd ignored packets more than Size ids behind the tail, but not one
exactly Size behind: in an empty window, a copy of the newest polled packet (offset 0). Steam
resends a reliable packet whenever our ACK for it is lost, so this happens often. The copy was
stored in the slot just freed, outside the window. When the window came round to that slot
again (128 packets later on the control channel) it read as taken, and the new packet there
was dropped, and every retransmission of it too, while the ACK stayed at the id before it.

A debug log on a Samsung QN85A showed Steam sending control packet 10311, its once-a-minute
HID GetFeatureReport, about 470 times a second for 18 s, all refused, with controller input
held the whole time. This was the periodic controller freeze (issue #3).

--- a/src/session/window.c	2026-11-23 08:23:51.469446309 +0100
+++ b/src/session/window.c	2026-11-23 08:23:51.475421722 +0100
@@ -79,8 +79,12 @@
 bool IHS_SessionPacketsWindowAdd(IHS_SessionPacketsWindow *window, IHS_SessionPacket *packet) {
     /* Calculate distance of 2 items */
     int tailOffset = window->tail.pos < 0 ? 1 : (int16_t) (packet->header.packetId - window->tail.id);
-    /* We already processed this packet, so ignore it */
-    if (tailOffset < 0 && -tailOffset > IHS_SessionPacketsWindowSize(window)) {
+    /* We already processed this packet, so ignore it. The window holds the last Size ids up to
+     * and including the tail; anything at or before tail - Size was polled. A copy of the newest
+     * polled packet (tailOffset 0 in an empty window: Steam resends it when our ACK is lost) used
+     * to pass here and land in the slot just freed, outside the window. A lap later that slot
+     * read as taken, and every copy of the packet due there was dropped. */
+    if (tailOffset <= 0 && -tailOffset >= IHS_SessionPacketsWindowSize(window)) {
         return true;
     }
     /* Further ahead than the window holds: the stream went on without us (on Wi-Fi, a gap of a few
